        <!-- SECTION 3: CONSUMER APPEAL -->
        <div id="consumer-appeal" class="nav-section">
            <div class="section">
                <h2>3. Consumer Appeal & Sentiment</h2>

                <h3>What Consumers Love About Frozen Yogurt</h3>
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8;">
                    <li><strong>Customization control.</strong> Self-serve model lets consumers build their own experience; toppings are highly Instagram-able.</li>
                    <li><strong>Health perception.</strong> Probiotics, lower fat content, and perceived wellness appeal—especially Gen Z and health-conscious adults.</li>
                    <li><strong>Freshness & variety.</strong> Rotating flavors (monthly LTOs), limited-edition seasonal offerings, regional adaptations (matcha, ube, regional fruits).</li>
                    <li><strong>Social experience.</strong> Pinkberry/Yogurtland/Menchie's became social hubs for young adults; "froyo date" culture.</li>
                    <li><strong>Portion flexibility.</strong> Pay-by-weight model appeals to health-conscious consumers wanting smaller servings.</li>
                    <li><strong>Flavor innovation.</strong> Asia-Pacific driving premiumization through unique flavors (Lalasweet Greek yogurt 76× YoY growth in South Korea in 2024).</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>What Consumers Love About Frozen Custard</h3>
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8;">
                    <li><strong>Texture & creaminess.</strong> Egg yolks create velvet-like density; "smoother, silkier than ice cream" per Culver's brand research.</li>
                    <li><strong>Indulgence.</strong> Perceived as premium, craft, small-batch. High-fat content (14% by definition) = richness consumers actively seek.</li>
                    <li><strong>Freshness commitment.</strong> Culver's "made fresh throughout the day in-house" positioning resonates—no frozen stockpiles, batch production.</li>
                    <li><strong>Loyalty & nostalgia.</strong> Culver's 40-year tenure shows custard builds transgenerational loyalty; families return weekly, recommend it to friends.</li>
                    <li><strong>Mix-in versatility.</strong> Concretes (Shake Shack) and mix-ins (Culver's) create personalization without self-serve chaos.</li>
                    <li><strong>Premium perception.</strong> Culver's $4.2M AUV (2025) proves consumers will pay for perceived quality; custard commands price premium over ice cream.</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="chart-container">
                    <canvas id="appealChart"></canvas>
                </div>

                <h3>What Consumers Complain About</h3>
                <div class="comparison-row">
                    <div class="comparison-item frozen-yogurt">
                        <strong>Frozen Yogurt Complaints</strong>
                        <ul>
                            <li>"Toppings make it unhealthy" (sugar, candy override yogurt benefit)</li>
                            <li>"Tastes sour/artificial" (quality variance between shops)</li>
                            <li>"Another froyo chain—nothing special"</li>
                            <li>"High prices for self-serve" (perception)</li>
                            <li>"Consistency issues between franchises"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="comparison-item frozen-custard">
                        <strong>Frozen Custard Complaints</strong>
                        <ul>
                            <li>"Rich but heavy" (one serving fills consumers)</li>
                            <li>"Seasonal availability" (Midwest chains close in winter)</li>
                            <li>"Limited flavor choice" (vs. frozen yogurt's 40+ options)</li>
                            <li>"Not perceived as healthy" (egg yolks, high fat)</li>
                            <li>"Geographic limitation" (Culver's only in 26 states)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="key-finding">
                    <strong>Consumer verdict:</strong> Frozen yogurt wins on "healthy" perception and novelty; frozen custard wins on "premium" perception and loyalty. Consumers don't compare them directly—frozen yogurt = healthy snack, frozen custard = indulgent treat. They occupy different consumption occasions.
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 4: BUSINESS MODELS -->
        <div id="business-models" class="nav-section">
            <div class="section">
                <h2>4. Business Model Comparison</h2>

                <h3>Frozen Yogurt Business Model</h3>
                <p><strong>Format:</strong> Self-serve, pay-by-weight. Customer loads cup, selects toppings, pays at checkout. Low labor at point-of-sale (vs. counter service).</p>
                <p><strong>Customization:</strong> 30+ toppings standard; unlimited flavor combinations. Highly Instagrammable.</p>
                <p><strong>Flavor rotation:</strong> Monthly LTO (limited-time offerings) drive repeat visits. Seasonal / regional flavors.</p>
                <p><strong>Positioning:</strong> Casual, fun, health-forward. Urban locations (malls, high-traffic areas).</p>
                <p><strong>Unit economics:</strong> 250–400 unit franchises (Yogurtland, Menchie's); average unit volumes lower than custard concepts (~$1.5–2.5M estimated).</p>
                <p><strong>Delivery/Digital:</strong> Strong online presence, digital toppings menus, Instagram strategy (user-generated content = free marketing).</p>

                <h3>Frozen Custard Business Model</h3>
                <p><strong>Format:</strong> Counter service, small-batch production. Crew scoops and hand-packs. Higher touch, craft positioning.</p>
                <p><strong>Customization:</strong> Signature Flavor of the Day (limited choice, exclusivity); mix-ins/concretes (staff-made per order).</p>
                <p><strong>Flavor rotation:</strong> Daily Flavor of the Day (Culver's model) creates urgency and drives repeat visits for "collector" behavior.</p>
                <p><strong>Positioning:</strong> Premium, craft, family-friendly (QSR bundled with burgers at Culver's, Freddy's, Shake Shack).</p>
                <p><strong>Unit economics:</strong> Culver's 1000+ units, $4.2M AUV (2025); Freddy's 600 units, growing. Higher transaction values (custard + burger/fries).</p>
                <p><strong>Delivery:</strong> Emerging (Culver's mobile ordering, curbside pickup); less Instagram-focused than frozen yogurt.</p>

                <div class="chart-container">
                    <canvas id="businessModelChart"></canvas>
                </div>

