        <!-- SECTION 5: CASE STUDIES -->
        <div id="case-studies" class="nav-section">
            <div class="section">
                <h2>5. International Case Studies</h2>

                <h3>Pinkberry (Frozen Yogurt, Global Leader)</h3>
                <div class="case-study">
                    <div class="case-study-title">Concept: Premium tart frozen yogurt + fresh fruit toppings</div>
                    <div class="case-study-detail">
                        Founded 2005 (Los Angeles). Minimal-design Apple-store–like aesthetic. Signature tart flavor (probiotics positioned as health benefit). Fresh fruit toppings, no candy. Premium brand equity score 9.1/10 (top quartile). Allows 15–20% price premium over competitors. Presence in high-traffic urban centers and luxury malls globally. Weakness: 2022 sales down 31.8% over 3-year average; unit count declining (market saturation, frozen yogurt trend fade).
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h3>Yogurtland (Frozen Yogurt, Franchisee Favorite)</h3>
                <div class="case-study">
                    <div class="case-study-title">Concept: Self-serve, pay-by-weight, 250+ locations</div>
                    <div class="case-study-detail">
                        Founded 2006 (California). Pioneered self-serve + pay-by-weight model. Proprietary rotating flavor system; 40+ options at any location. Strong digital strategy (Instagram UGC, TikTok). Adaptable to regional tastes (mango in Asia, tropical in tropical markets). Recent moves: dairy-free partnerships (Oatly), plant-based lines. Growth is stable but not explosive; premium brand positioning works in affluent neighborhoods.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h3>Culver's (Frozen Custard, Gold Standard)</h3>
                <div class="case-study">
                    <div class="case-study-title">Concept: ButterBurgers + Fresh Frozen Custard, daily Flavor of the Day</div>
                    <div class="case-study-detail">
                        Founded 1984 (Wisconsin). 1000+ company-owned and franchised restaurants across 26 states. $4.2M average unit volume (2025)—the highest among dessert-focused QSRs. Fresh custard made on-site throughout the day (competitive advantage: no freezer stock). Flavor of the Day creates urgency and collectibility. Strong customer loyalty (families visit 2–3x/week); employee satisfaction high (Midwest hospitality culture). Burger bundling critical to AUV. Expansion strategy: slow and controlled, prioritizing unit quality. International expansion minimal (cultural Midwest attachment).
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h3>Freddy's Frozen Custard & Steakburgers (Custard, Growth Challenger)</h3>
                <div class="case-study">
                    <div class="case-study-title">Concept: Steakburgers + crinkle fries + frozen custard, 600 locations</div>
                    <div class="case-study-detail">
                        Founded 2002 (Kansas). Rapid franchisee-driven expansion (~60 locations/year). $475M revenue (2020s). Opening in new markets (Northeast, Florida, Pacific Northwest). Inline and end-cap store formats expanding (lower capex than standalone). 12.9% sales growth year-over-year (competing with Culver's). Burger-custard bundling same as Culver's. Recent focus: digital ordering, POS infrastructure, app development. International pilot ongoing (Canada expansion).
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h3>Shake Shack (Frozen Custard as Menu Component)</h3>
                <div class="case-study">
                    <div class="case-study-title">Concept: Premium burgers + shakes + frozen custard, global presence</div>
                    <div class="case-study-detail">
                        Founded 2004 (via Danny Meyer's Union Square Hospitality). 400+ locations globally (USA, Japan, UK, Middle East). Frozen custard is 10–15% of menu revenue; shakes are the hero product. Custard made fresh daily, scooped to order. Concretes and seasonal flavor calendars drive repeat visits. International adaptation: cherry blossom shake (Japan), red bean (Korea), pandan (Singapore). Premium positioning (higher check average than QSR burger competitors). Expansion via company-owned + licensed model. Custard is supporting player to burgers, not primary driver.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h3>Lalasweet (Frozen Yogurt, Asia Premiumization Champion)</h3>
                <div class="case-study">
                    <div class="case-study-title">Concept: Greek-yogurt ice cream, Korea, 76x growth in 4 months</div>
                    <div class="case-study-detail">
                        South Korea market. Positioned as "affordable luxury" (KRW 3,000 vs. KRW 1,500 standard). Greek yogurt base (higher protein, premium perception). Sold 4.4M units in first four months of 2024—76× year-over-year growth. Reflects broader Asia trend: premiumization + health positioning + regional innovation driving frozen yogurt adoption faster than Western markets.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="key-finding">
                    <strong>Case study synthesis:</strong>
                    <br/>• Frozen yogurt leaders (Pinkberry, Yogurtland) succeeded on innovation + Instagram, but face declining unit counts and category saturation.
                    <br/>• Frozen custard leaders (Culver's, Freddy's) achieve higher AUV, stronger loyalty, and steadier growth by bundling with burgers or creating product scarcity (daily Flavor of the Day).
