                <h2>8. Strategic Recommendation</h2>

                <h3>The Case for Frozen Custard</h3>
                <p>Based on the evidence, <strong>Frozen Custard is the recommended entry strategy for your premium brand in Casablanca.</strong></p>

                <p><strong>Why:</strong></p>
                <ol style="margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.9;">
                    <li><strong>Zero category competition in Morocco.</strong> Frozen custard is genuinely absent from the market. This is not a weakness—it is a competitive moat. You can own the category definition and become the customer's reference point for premium frozen custard.</li>
                    <li><strong>Superior differentiation.</strong> Frozen yogurt differentiates on flavors and toppings—which competitors can copy. Frozen custard differentiates on <em>the product itself</em>—the texture, richness, craftsmanship, daily-fresh positioning. This is harder to copy and builds deeper brand attachment.</li>
                    <li><strong>Exceptional loyalty potential.</strong> Culver's 40-year track record, $4.2M AUV, and 2–3x weekly frequency prove that frozen custard builds transgenerational, family-based loyalty. Frozen yogurt's declining unit counts (Pinkberry, Menchie's) suggest shallower loyalty.</li>
                    <li><strong>Premium positioning alignment.</strong> Your brand concept ("Le conseil avant la transaction" / advice before transaction) suggests a high-touch, quality-focused approach. Frozen custard's craft positioning and made-fresh ethos align perfectly with this. Frozen yogurt is self-serve and transactional.</li>
                    <li><strong>Global trend alignment.</strong> Premiumization is accelerating globally (Asia-Pacific, North America). Consumers are willing to pay more for perceived quality, craft, and health attributes. Frozen custard (especially with smart ingredient sourcing—local dairy, probiotic cultures, clean label positioning) can capture this trend.</li>
                    <li><strong>Runw for expansion.</strong> If successful in Casablanca, a custard concept can expand to Rabat, Marrakech, and internationally (Middle East, Europe) far more easily than another froyo shop. Culver's and Freddy's international playbooks prove this.</li>
                </ol>

                <h3>Recommended Entry Strategy: Hybrid/Phased Model</h3>
                <p><strong>Phase 1 (Year 1–18 months):</strong> Premium frozen custard as the core product. Position as "the craft alternative to mass-market ice cream." Daily Flavor of the Day strategy (create urgency and collectibility). Concretes/mix-ins for customization. High-quality local sourcing (Moroccan dairy partners, seasonal ingredients). Singular, flagship location in high-traffic Casablanca locale (business district or affluent residential area).</p>

                <p><strong>Phase 2 (Year 2+):</strong> Once custard brand is established, consider limited frozen yogurt offerings as a summer or health-conscious variant (dairy-free option, lower-fat alternative). This is *not* rebranding to froyo; it's a supporting menu item that allows you to capture off-peak occasions (summer, health seekers) without diluting the custard brand.</p>

                <p><strong>Why hybrid?</strong> Pure frozen custard is your competitive advantage; pure frozen yogurt is a commoditized category. A hybrid approach lets you own custard and use yogurt as a supporting product for specific occasions, not as a co-equal brand pillar.</p>

                <div class="recommendation-box">
                    <div class="recommendation-title">If You Choose Frozen Yogurt Instead</div>
                    <p>Frozen yogurt is a viable, lower-risk alternative if your priority is operational simplicity and immediate scalability. Execute with:</p>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li>Premium positioning (not mass-market; avoid generic "froyo")</li>
                        <li>Limited but high-quality toppings (fresh fruit, nuts, artisanal sauces—not candy)</li>
