        <div id="testing" class="nav-section">
            <div class="section">
                <h2>9. Casablanca Validation Plan: Before You Invest</h2>

                <p>Do not make the final investment decision based solely on this desk research. The following validation plan is essential.</p>

                <h3>Phase 1: Concept Testing (Weeks 1–4)</h3>
                <p><strong>Objective:</strong> Test frozen custard vs. frozen yogurt appeal in your target Casablanca market.</p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li><strong>Blind taste test:</strong> Recruit 60–80 target consumers (ages 18–45, affluent urban Casablanca, active on social media). Serve blind samples of: (A) premium frozen custard, (B) premium frozen yogurt, (C) high-end ice cream. Ask for taste preference, texture preference, purchase intent, willingness to pay.</li>
                    <li><strong>Concept cards:</strong> Show each consumer brand positioning cards for "craft frozen custard" vs. "premium custom froyo." Measure purchase intent, brand perception, and price sensitivity.</li>
                    <li><strong>Occasion mapping:</strong> Identify when consumers would choose each option (social outing, family dessert, personal treat, summer vs. winter).</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Phase 2: Location & Demographic Viability (Weeks 3–8)</h3>
                <p><strong>Objective:</strong> Validate target market size, foot traffic, and location potential.</p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li><strong>Site analysis:</strong> Scout 3–5 potential Casablanca locations (high-traffic commercial, affluent residential nearby). Measure foot traffic patterns, demographic composition, rent assumptions, competitor proximity.</li>
                    <li><strong>Demographic segmentation:</strong> Quantify addressable market in target neighborhoods (25–45 age range, upper-middle income, college educated). Use local census data + consumer surveys.</li>
                    <li><strong>Tourism flow:</strong> Validate international visitor patterns to Casablanca and likelihood of trial.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Phase 3: Flavor & Product Adaptation Testing (Weeks 5–12)</h3>
                <p><strong>Objective:</strong> Test Moroccan flavor appeal and product adaptation.</p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li><strong>Flavor co-creation:</strong> Work with local ingredient suppliers (dairy, fruit, spice producers). Develop 5–7 Moroccan-inspired custard flavors (date, pistachio, amlou, orange blossom, honey, saffron, argan). Test with consumer panel.</li>
                    <li><strong>Ingredient sourcing:</strong> Validate local dairy sourcing (milk, cream, eggs). Identify cold-chain partners for logistics.</li>
                    <li><strong>Production feasibility:</strong> Mini-batch production in a local kitchen or partnership facility. Prove daily-fresh production model is operationally viable.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Phase 4: Pop-Up Pilot (Weeks 10–16)</h3>
                <p><strong>Objective:</strong> Test full concept in market with limited risk.</p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li><strong>Pop-up location:</strong> Open a temporary frozen custard stand or cart in a high-traffic Casablanca location (mall, commercial district, university) for 4–6 weeks.</li>
                    <li><strong>Measure:</strong> Daily/weekly sales volume, customer demographics, repeat rate, basket size, flavor preferences, social media engagement (Instagram tagging, shares), customer feedback, willingness to recommend.</li>
                    <li><strong>Pricing test:</strong> A/B test price points (DH 50 vs. DH 75 for standard serving) to determine price elasticity.</li>
                    <li><strong>Win/loss analysis:</strong> Why did customers choose your concept over ice cream or nothing? What would make them visit more frequently?</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Decision Checkpoint: Proceed or Pivot?</h3>
                <p><strong>Green light (proceed to flagship store):</strong></p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li>Blind taste test shows frozen custard significantly preferred over frozen yogurt (60%+ preference).</li>
                    <li>Pop-up sales exceed 300+ units per week at target price point.</li>
                    <li>Repeat customer rate ≥20% (indicates loyalty potential).</li>
                    <li>Average transaction value ≥ projection (price acceptance validated).</li>
                    <li>Social media engagement strong (organic tagging, word-of-mouth indicators).</li>
                </ul>

                <p><strong>Amber light (pilot longer or adjust):</strong></p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li>Custard vs. yogurt preference is marginal or mixed.</li>
                    <li>Pop-up sales are solid but not exceptional (150–250 units/week).</li>
                    <li>Repeat rate is lower than expected (5–10%).</li>
                    <li>Price resistance at target point; demand stronger at lower price.</li>
                    <li>Consider hybrid frozen custard + frozen yogurt model to capture broader market.</li>
                </ul>

                <p><strong>Red light (pivot or abort):</strong></p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li>Frozen custard shows no advantage over frozen yogurt or ice cream in blind test.</li>
                    <li>Pop-up sales underperform (≤100 units/week at target price).</li>
                    <li>Repeat rate negligible (&lt;5%).</li>
                    <li>Strong price resistance even at discounted levels.</li>
                    <li>Social media engagement minimal; no organic word-of-mouth.</li>
                    <li>Action: Pivot to frozen yogurt model, different location, different positioning, or abort dessert concept.</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="success">
                    <strong>Critical success factor:</strong> Do not skip the pop-up phase. A 4–6 week pilot costs far less than a flagship build-out and will answer your most critical questions before you commit real capital.
                </div>
