                <h2>10. The Decision</h2>

                <div class="decision-frame" style="border: none; padding: 0;">
                    <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">What the World Tells Us</h3>
                    <p>Globally, frozen yogurt is a larger, more mature category with strong growth in Asia-Pacific, driven by premiumization and regional flavors. Frozen custard is smaller, North America-concentrated, but shows exceptional loyalty metrics (Culver's $4.2M AUV, 40-year retention) and steady growth (5.9% CAGR).</p>

                    <h3>What Consumers Want</h3>
                    <p>Frozen yogurt consumers want health, customization, and novelty. Frozen custard consumers want indulgence, quality, and daily-fresh craftsmanship. The two categories serve different occasions and psychographics—they do not directly compete; they are complementary.</p>

                    <h3>What Frozen Yogurt Does Better</h3>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li>Perceived health benefit (lower fat, probiotics)</li>
                        <li>Customization and self-serve appeal</li>
                        <li>Operational simplicity and unit replicability</li>
                        <li>Social media virality (Instagram-friendly)</li>
                        <li>Global consumer familiarity</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h3>What Frozen Custard Does Better</h3>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li>Product differentiation (texture, richness, unique)</li>
                        <li>Premium perception and price power</li>
                        <li>Repeat-purchase frequency and loyalty</li>
                        <li>Brand attachment (not commodity)</li>
                        <li>Category ownership potential (Morocco = white space)</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h3>Why Morocco is Different</h3>
                    <p>No frozen-custard category exists in Morocco (competitive white space). Frozen yogurt has minimal presence. The market is receptive to premium frozen desserts but price-conscious on commodity offerings. Young urban population with social media adoption creates opportunity for viral brand building. Success requires category education for custard, but reward is ownership, not competition in an existing category.</p>

                    <h3>Biggest Opportunity</h3>
                    <p>Frozen custard: You can define the category in Morocco before competitors arrive. Global precedent (Culver's, Freddy's) proves it works. Once established, custard brand becomes defensible—hard to copy, high loyalty, premium positioning sustainable. Expansion to other Moroccan cities and MENA region becomes natural next step.</p>

                    <h3>Biggest Risk</h3>
                    <p>Frozen custard: Consumer education required. If execution is poor (location, quality, positioning), the unique product alone will not save you. Must get the full experience right. Pop-up pilot is essential. Also, higher operational complexity and quality-dependence mean franchisee selection is critical.</p>

                    <h3>Recommended Concept</h3>
                    <p><strong>Premium Frozen Custard</strong> with supporting frozen yogurt options (summer variant, dairy-free tier) after Year 1, if market validation supports it.</p>

                    <h3>What Must Be Validated Before Investing</h3>
                    <ol style="margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li>Blind taste test: Frozen custard ≥60% preference over frozen yogurt + ice cream.</li>
                        <li>Pop-up pilot: ≥300 units/week at target price point; ≥20% repeat rate.</li>
                        <li>Moroccan flavor appeal: Local ingredient sourcing validated; 5+ original flavors tested and refined.</li>
                        <li>Location viability: Target neighborhoods have sufficient affluent, digitally-engaged 18–45 demographic.</li>
                        <li>Operational feasibility: Daily-fresh production model proved in Casablanca partnership facility.</li>
                    </ol>

                    <div style="margin-top: 28px; padding: 20px; background: #f5f9ff; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #2a78d6;">
                        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.6;"><strong>Final Recommendation:</strong></p>
                        <p style="margin: 12px 0 0; font-size: 16px;"><span style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px;">Frozen Custard</span> is the recommended entry strategy because it offers zero-competition category ownership, superior differentiation, exceptional loyalty potential, and alignment with global premiumization trends—but only proceed after validating consumer appeal and operational feasibility through the phased pop-up pilot described above.</p>
                    </div>
